# Architecture

## What I am learning

How to structure software so it can evolve: modularity, boundaries, dependency direction, and the trade-offs between monoliths, modular monoliths, microservices, and event-driven designs.

## Why it matters

Code that works today but cannot change tomorrow becomes expensive. Architecture is the set of decisions that are hard to reverse, so it pays to make them deliberately and to record why. This topic also protects against a common trap: splitting an application into microservices before understanding the cost.

> **Goal:** structure systems that need to evolve, and explain the trade-offs behind each decision.

> **Important principle:** microservices are not automatically better architecture. The project first shows that a modular monolith can be designed properly before any distributed service is introduced.

---

## Learning objectives

By the end of this topic I can:

- Explain coupling and cohesion, and recognize both in real code
- Apply separation of concerns and the SOLID principles, and know when they are overkill
- Explain dependency inversion and use it to isolate business logic from infrastructure
- Compare layered, hexagonal, and clean architecture
- Design a modular monolith with explicit module boundaries
- Explain microservices, what they cost, and when they are justified
- Explain event-driven architecture and its failure modes
- Explain domain-driven design fundamentals: bounded contexts, entities, value objects, aggregates
- Document a decision as an ADR with context, decision, alternatives, and consequences
- Evaluate an existing design and identify its main architectural risks

---

